   "stem": {
      "text": "Evaluate \\( \\lim_{x \\to \\pi/4} \\dfrac{1 - \\tan x}{\\sin x - \\cos x} \\). Show the limits of the numerator and of the denominator before rewriting, and give an exact value."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Negate",
         [
            "Sqrt",
            2
         ]
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "As x approaches pi/4 the numerator approaches 1 - tan(pi/4) = 0 and the denominator approaches sqrt(2)/2 - sqrt(2)/2 = 0, so the form is zero over zero.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Write tan x = sin x / cos x, so 1 - tan x = (cos x - sin x)/cos x = -(sin x - cos x)/cos x.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Negate",
               [
                  "Subtract",
                  [
                     "Sin",
                     "x"
                  ],
                  [
                     "Cos",
                     "x"
                  ]
               ]
            ],
            [
               "Cos",
               "x"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Dividing by sin x - cos x, which is not zero near pi/4 except at pi/4, the common factor divides out and leaves -1/cos x.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            -1,
            [
               "Cos",
               "x"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "At x = pi/4 this is -1/(sqrt(2)/2) = -sqrt(2).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Sqrt",
